Viv from LangChain shared an interview question on X. Design a Q&A agent over a folder of markdown, PDFs, images, and video.

I asked my coding agent, which has the Pixeltable skill, to write candidates a cheatsheet for it. Full disclosure, I work on Pixeltable, a multimodal backend. I assured the agent that plugging Pixeltable in interviews was ok 😄

1. How would a ReadFile() tool work per file type?
- Each file type needs its own conversion, and video and images take the most work.
- Do that work once when files come in, not on every question.
- Use infrastructure that's natively multimodal, so you don't build a separate stack per file type.
Plug #1: guess which one I'd point you to. Frames, clips, and embeddings are built in, so there's no ffmpeg job to write.

2. How would you design the search harness? What if we care most about cost, or about recall?
- Search everything you prepared, and return each hit with a short preview and its page or timestamp.
- For cost, good previews keep the context window small.
- For recall, combine keyword, embedding, and frame search, since each misses things the others catch.
Plug #2: all of those searches run over the same files in one database, so every result points back to the same file and time, whichever search found it.

3. What about A LOT of video?
- Preprocess once, with timestamped transcripts and frames at the rate the model expects. Gemini's default is one frame per second.
- Avoid sending raw video at question time. Gemini counts about 100 tokens per second of video at default settings, so it adds up fast.
Plug #3: each video is transcribed and embedded once, and adding more only processes the new ones.

4. Which models?
- Use different models for search and for answering.
- For search, use a video embedding model like Twelve Labs when motion or sound matters, or CLIP on frames when cost or privacy matters, since it's free and runs locally.
- For answering, use a model that takes video, like Gemini, on the top few clips.
Plug #4: hosted or local, each model is one function call, so you can compare Twelve Labs and CLIP on the same videos.

5. The data is sports events. Most people ask "when" something happened, and some ask "how." How would you design for both?
- "When" is usually a moment in a match. Commentary and the on-screen scoreboard catch score-based moments, and video embeddings on short clips catch visual ones.
- "How" spans a stretch of the match, so summarize each set ahead of time and have the model cite timestamps.
Plug #5: every search result keeps its time in the video, which answers "when" and points to the footage for "how."

6. Context window or code?
- Use code for mechanical work, like counting how many documents mention a name, and pass only the result to the model.
- Use the context window for work that takes understanding, like explaining a scene from its transcript and clips.
Plug #6: the queries you write are also tools the model can call, so it can run that count itself.

